\name{InferenceAbstractKKOrdinalCLMM}
\alias{InferenceAbstractKKOrdinalCLMM}
\title{Abstract class for ordinal CLMM-based Inference in KK designs}
\description{
The random-intercept standard deviation \eqn{\sigma_b} may be estimated at
the lower boundary (\eqn{\sigma_b \to 0}, reported as a log-sigma near
\eqn{-5}). That is a legitimate maximum-likelihood estimate when the
likelihood decreases as \eqn{\sigma_b^2} leaves 0; the fit then equals the
fixed-effects cumulative-link fit.

The native fit maximizes the marginal likelihood from several starts (the
default start and a few values of \eqn{\log\sigma_b}), polishes the best one
and judges convergence by the projected gradient: its norm must be at most
\eqn{\max(10^{-5}, \epsilon_g)} (\eqn{\epsilon_g} is the kernel tolerance, \code{1e-12}
here), where at the lower variance boundary the
\eqn{\log\sigma_b} coordinate is left out when the score in
\eqn{\sigma_b^2} points outward (a KKT point). When that test fails after
L-BFGS, damped Newton steps are taken on the remaining coordinates.

A native fit is accepted only if it converged and the gradient norm is at
most \eqn{10^{-4}}. Otherwise it is retried once with Newton-Raphson from the
returned parameters (from that point only, unless the fit sits at the lower
variance boundary with a gradient norm above \eqn{10^{-3}}, where the
\eqn{\log\sigma_b} starts are rerun as well); if that also fails the gradient
test, the estimate is \code{NA} with nonestimable reason
\code{"kk_clmm_rcpp_not_stationary"}. With the default tolerance the retry
rarely runs (at large n, when Newton reaches the numerical-Hessian floor
between \eqn{10^{-5}} and \eqn{10^{-4}}).

\code{compute_rand_two_sided_pval(type = "score")} is an opt-in
randomization statistic for large \eqn{n}. The default statistic refits the
CLMM on every redraw (about a second per redraw at \eqn{n = 1000}). The
score statistic fits the CLMM once with the treatment coefficient fixed at
0 and takes, for each subject, the posterior mean over its pair's random
intercept of \eqn{\partial \log p(y_i \mid \eta_i) / \partial \eta_i}; the
statistic is \eqn{T(w) = \sum_i w_i r_i}, the score for the treatment
coefficient, so each redraw is one inner product (a p-value with 501 draws
takes well under a second at \eqn{n = 1000}). It uses the same draws, tail
counting, tie rule and \eqn{(1 + b)/(1 + B)} p-value as the default. Like any
randomization test it is exact under the sharp null of no effect, but it is
a different statistic, so its p-values and power differ (simulated, KK14,
\eqn{n = 50}, a 50\%-power effect: rejection rate 0.43 against 0.37 for the
default). It tests only \eqn{\delta = 0}. These classes do not offer a
randomization confidence interval.

The \pkg{ordinal} package's \code{clmm()} (used by the weighted
Bayesian-bootstrap refits and by \code{use_rcpp = FALSE}) fits the log-log
model when asked for \code{link = "cloglog"} and vice versa (observed in
\pkg{ordinal} 2026.7.26; \code{clm()} is correct). EDI detects this once per
session with a small probe and passes \code{clmm()} the link name that fits
the complementary log-log model.
}
